ADR-001: Merge Strategy — Paperclip + Hermes Agent
Date: 2026-03-26 Status: Accepted Decision makers: Oussama Ammar, Claude (AI architect)

Context
The Hana Sachiko Company, Inc. needs an internal operating system to run an AI-native IP studio. Two open-source projects provide complementary capabilities:

Paperclip (TypeScript) — agent orchestration, task management, org structure, React dashboard
Hermes Agent (Python) — self-improving AI agent with skills, memory, learning, messaging gateway
Neither project alone meets our needs. We evaluated three approaches to combine them.

Decision
Approach C: Hybrid Monorepo — Both projects live side-by-side in one repository, connected via API and the adapter system.

Alternatives Considered
Approach A: Paperclip-First
Paperclip as base, Hermes as a subprocess adapter only.

Rejected because: Treats Hermes as a black box. Loses the ability to deeply integrate the intelligence layer with all agents, not just sovereign ones.
Approach B: Hermes-First
Hermes as base, Paperclip's orchestration rewritten in Python.

Rejected because: Paperclip's server is 15k+ lines of battle-tested TypeScript. Rewriting it in Python would take months and lose the existing adapter system, plugin system, and UI integration. No working system for a very long time.
Approach C: Hybrid Monorepo (Selected)
Both projects in one repo. Paperclip handles orchestration (tasks, org, budgets, UI). Hermes handles intelligence (skills, memory, learning, messaging). Connected via adapters and MCP.

Selected because:
Each layer does what it's best at
Existing adapters (claude_local, codex_local) continue working immediately
Hermes' intelligence is accessible to ALL agents, not just Hermes-native ones
Docker Compose makes multi-runtime (Node.js + Python) trivial
Incremental: dashboard + Claude/Codex agents can work in days, Hermes learning layered over time
Module system maps perfectly to MCP — cleanly separated, independently developable
Consequences
Two runtimes (Node.js + Python) must be managed — Docker handles this
Inter-service communication adds latency — acceptable for our use case
Engineers need familiarity with both TypeScript and Python
Clear separation of concerns prevents the codebases from entangling